DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     sseellddrraaiinn(), sseellrreeccoorrdd() and sseellwwaakkeeuupp() are the three central functions
     used by select(2), poll(2) and the objects that are being selected on.
     They handle the task of recording which threads are waiting on which ob‐
     jects and the waking of the proper threads when an event of interest oc‐
     curs on an object.

     sseellrreeccoorrdd() records that the calling thread is interested in events re‐
     lated to a given object.  If another thread is already waiting on the ob‐
     ject a collision will be flagged in _s_i_p which will be later dealt with by
     sseellwwaakkeeuupp().

     sseellrreeccoorrdd() acquires and releases _s_e_l_l_o_c_k.

     sseellwwaakkeeuupp() is called by the underlying object handling code in order to
     notify any waiting threads that an event of interest has occurred.  If a
     collision has occurred, sseellwwaakkeeuupp() will increment _n_s_e_l_c_o_l_l, and broad‐
     cast on the global cv in order to wake all waiting threads so that they
     can handle it.  If the thread waiting on the object is not currently
     sleeping or the wait channel is not _s_e_l_w_a_i_t, sseellwwaakkeeuupp() will clear the
     TDF_SELECT flag which should be noted by select(2) and poll(2) when they
     wake up.

     sseellddrraaiinn() will flush the waiters queue on a specified object before its
     destruction.  The object handling code must ensure that _*_s_i_p cannot be
     used once sseellddrraaiinn() has been called.

     The contents of _*_s_i_p must be zeroed, such as by softc initialization, be‐
     fore any call to sseellrreeccoorrdd() or sseellwwaakkeeuupp(), otherwise a panic may occur.
     sseellwwaakkeeuupp() acquires and releases _s_e_l_l_o_c_k and may acquire and release
     _s_c_h_e_d___l_o_c_k.  sseellddrraaiinn() could usually be just a wrapper for sseellwwaakkeeuupp(),
     but consumers should not generally rely on this feature.

